text:
maya: FYI customer reported a sudden billing spike. They see duplicate POSTs from our SDK after a timeout + retry. Suspect interaction between AbortController and backoff in Node20/global fetch. Repro inside.

support-bot: Thanks, ticket #CS-4721 created and assigned to @maya. :eyes:

chris: Which SDK version and do they use our bundled undici or node's global fetch?

maya: TS SDK 1.4.2, Node 20.5.0. No explicit undici in their deps — relying on node's global fetch. They show undici in traces on our infra though (server logs show undici user-agent).

devon: Can you paste a minimal repro? Even a short snippet helps.

maya: Repro they gave (simplified):
```js
import fetch from 'node-fetch'; // they actually use global fetch in prod, but repro used node-fetch
const payload = { order: 123, items: [1,2,3] }

async function sendWithRetry() {
  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    const ctrl = new AbortController();
    const t = setTimeout(() => ctrl.abort(), 400); // intended per-request timeout
    try {
      console.log('attempt', i);
      const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.com/submit', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify(payload),
        signal: ctrl.signal
      });
      clearTimeout(t);
      if (res.ok) return res.json();
    } catch (e) {
      clearTimeout(t);
      console.log('err', e.name || e.message);
      // naive backoff
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100 * Math.pow(2, i)));
    }
  }
}

sendWithRetry();
```

maya: Server logs show the same request body received twice in quick succession, and our billing system counted both. Customer says the first request timed out client-side, then second one succeeded.

chris: Two likely causes: (1) body stream was consumed and the client retried with a clone created by fetch internals (rare), or (2) connection/timeout semantics caused undici/global fetch to abort after the request was already sent but before response arrived, so server still processed it, then retry sent new request.

devon: Important detail: JSON.stringify(payload) is a string body — that should be replayable. Duplicate POSTs are expected if the first request actually reached server but client timed out waiting for response. We need to see timing and request ids to confirm.

maya: Attached server side trace (sanitized):
```
[2026-03-15T14:03:21Z] req_id=abc123 received POST /submit content-length=56
[2026-03-15T14:03:21Z] processing started req_id=abc123
[2026-03-15T14:03:22Z] completed 200 req_id=abc123 duration=1200ms
[2026-03-15T14:03:22Z] req_id=def456 received POST /submit content-length=56
```

maya: Client logs show attempt 0, err AbortError after ~400ms, then attempt 1 succeeds ~1600ms later. So the first actually took >400ms server-side (1200ms) and client aborted earlier, then retried and succeeded.

sre-jen: That matches: aborting the fetch does not guarantee the server didn't receive the request. The safe approaches: idempotency keys on POSTs or ensure server dedupes. We should recommend adding an idempotency header (x-idempotency-key) from client.

chris: Also check SDK behavior: older SDK versions had different retry wiring where AbortSignal propagation to underlying undici request could cancel but connection may still stream. If they're on a version that polyfills fetch with undici differently, a race can occur.

devon: Quick diagnostic steps for customer: (1) reproduce with Node 18 (no global fetch) or pin undici to a known version, (2) add an 'x-forwarded-trace' on server to correlate client attempts, (3) switch body to a Buffer to rule out stream/clone issues: `body: Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(payload))`.

maya: Customer tried Buffer and still saw duplicates. They confirmed server processed first request. So it's definitely a timeout vs server processing time mismatch.

chris: For mitigation: recommend increasing per-request abort timeout > expected server processing time, or implement idempotency keys. Also we can add guidance in our SDK docs explaining non-idempotent POSTs and retries.

maya: Action items:
- support: send repro to eng-ml/eng-runtime so they can test with our runtime and undici combos. @devon to run a containerized repro with node20 + global fetch.
- devex/docs: add a note on idempotency and aborttimeout interplay.
- ops: check if we can expose request-level request-id headers to customers (we already return x-redwood-request-id, but make it easier to echo).

devon: I'll run repro now. If I can reproduce duplicate server receives when client aborts before server completes, that's expected behavior. We should document it. Also will try toggling `signal: undefined` to verify behaviour.

sre-jen: Also note billing: we can post-process to detect near-duplicate identical payloads within a short window and mark as potential duplicates for manual review, but that's a bandaid. Proper fix is idempotency or conservative aborts.

maya: Thanks team. I'll loop back to the customer with recommended mitigations and ask them to add an idempotency key while we investigate runtime specifics.

support-bot: Update posted to ticket #CS-4721: "advise idempotency header + increase per-request timeout; engineering to reproduce in node20/undici combos." :white_check_mark:

chris: One more note — if they use streaming bodies (file uploads) then retries are trickier; recommend presigned URLs for uploads to avoid double-processing.

maya: Closing thread for now, will reopen if eng finds a runtime bug.
